<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Germani Cisrhenani</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cisrhenane Germans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Germani Cisrhenani were a group of peoples living west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> region whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> called Germani. They included the Eburones, Condrusi, Caeroesi, Paemani and Segni. Caesar met them during his Gallic campaigns of 57 to 53 BCE, and their name may lie behind the later general term Germani.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name means the Germani on this side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, that is, the western or Roman side. They lived west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhine">Lower Rhine</a> in what is now the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the northern German Rhineland. Julius Caesar understood them to descend from settlers who had crossed from east of the Rhine before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbrian-war">Cimbrian War</a> of the second century BCE, and he treated them as a group distinct from the neighbouring Belgae.</p><p>Their language is uncertain. They may have spoken a Celtic language rather than a Germanic one, and the name Germani itself is thought to be Gaulish in origin. Scholars have proposed two main explanations of their arrival: a very early migration, perhaps as early as Urnfield times, or a common origin of both Belgae and Germani in the Hunsrück-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a> culture near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Caesar's informants among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remi">Remi</a> told him before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-sabis">Battle of the Sabis</a> in 57 BCE that the tribes of the extreme north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, were called by the common name of Germani and sent one joint force to the Belgic alliance. The Aduatuci, descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a>, lived close by but were not counted among them.</p><p>When the revolt of 54 BCE broke out, Caesar named all the Germani on this side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> among the rebels, and the Eburones under their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a> led it. After the Eburones were defeated, the Segni and Condrusi sent envoys asking not to be treated as enemies, saying that they had sent Ambiorix no help.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Writing around 100 CE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> said that the people who had first crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and driven out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> were by his day called the Tungri, and that the name Germani had spread from them to many other peoples. Scholars have long debated the passage. Many now conclude that Caesar used Germani in a strict sense for these <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> tribes and in a wider sense for peoples of similar supposed ancestry east of the river.</p><p>Later Roman writers kept the local meaning: the two provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and Germania Superior with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, gave it official form, and the Civitas Tungrorum became part of Germania Inferior.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Germani cisrhenani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 57 BCE they promised, together with the other <b>Germani Cisrhenani</b>, some forty thousand warriors to the Belgic league against Caesar.</blockquote><a href="/state/caeroesi">Caeroesi</a></li><li><blockquote>Caesar counts them among the <b>Germani Cisrhenani</b> and says they had paid tribute to the Aduatuci.</blockquote><a href="/state/eburones">Eburones</a></li></ul></section>
